  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 1225 AM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nThe latest global model ensembles now have this batch of rain \nsticking around through Monday evening. As previously mentioned we \ncould see some heavier embedded cells in this batch of rain that \nwould be capable of producing heavier rainfall rates across \nlocations that are already saturated with earlier rains during the \nevent. WPC highlights a level 1 of 4 risk for flooding across parts \nof the Coastal plains and up into the majority of the San Antonio \nmetro.\n\nAs for amounts, several hi-res models have come in suggesting \nslightly higher totals with the rain sticking around later into \nTuesday. Thus we should see fairly widespread totals in the 1 to 4 \ninch range with some isolated pockets of up to 6 inches not out of \nthe question for areas that see repeated rounds of rainfall. \nHowever, almost everyone should see at least a half inch to an inch \nwhen all is said and done. These totals may seem like a lot but we \narent expecting any heavy torrential downpours but rather some good \nold fashioned steady cool rain that will occur over a period of \nseveral days rather than all at once.\n\nThe main upper level low eventually exits to the northeast towards \nTuesday but guidance continues to keep lingering shortwave energy \nbehind the system through Tuesday into Tuesday night. This and still \nthe presence of some coastal troughing to the south help to keep in \nsome low end pops (20-30%) across the area, favoring along and east \nof the I-35 corridor.\n\nWednesday into Thursday a longwave trough then becomes established \nstretching from the Ohio Valley into the Mississippi valley. \nEventually shortwave energy will advance southeastward from the \nCentral plains into East Texas/Texarkana region on the western \nperiphery of aforementioned troughing. This should help advance a \nweak cold front across our region at some point either Wednesday or \nWednesday night. The northwest flow that occurs behind this front \nwill bring in some drier air and finally push any lingering rain \nchances over our eastern areas more to the southeast and out of our \narea by Wednesday night.\n\nRegarding high temps, Tuesday we should see highs rebound slightly \nback into the low 70s for those that see breaks in the clouds and \nsome more sunshine. However, by Wednesday we begin to return to more \nnormal values for this time of year with everyone getting into the \nlow to mid 70s. Thursday as the potential front begins to move \nthrough, we should see highs drop back a couple of degrees into the \nlow 70s with this trend continuing through Friday and into the \nweekend. Lows will trend slightly cooler with many seeing mid 50s \nmidweek before several areas taste there first 40s of the season to \nbegin the weekend.",
